From: Wayland Cursor Fix <local@localhost>
Date: Mon, 03 Aug 2026 21:15:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] xwayland: grace window for warp+ungrab release invalidation

At high input rates (8 kHz-capable devices drawn at ~1 kHz), relative
motion which physically lands inside the sub-millisecond window between
WarpPointer and UngrabPointer request processing invalidates the frozen
release candidate introduced by revision 8. The release branch then
falls back to the moved sprite position, restoring the cursor a few
accelerated deltas past the intended target: direction-preserving,
speed-correlated reset misses on menu transitions.

Relative events carry compositor dispatch timestamps, not request-order
information, so in-flight delivery jitter also falsely reads as newer.
Motion within 8 ms of the candidate warp cannot be meaningfully newer
than a back-to-back pair: treat it as part of the pair. Capture-mode
recenter warps (every frame, 16+ ms apart) are unaffected; their
candidates are still invalidated by the continuous motion that follows.

Reproduced on KWin 6.6.4 with a 1 kHz portal injector and the
race-client harness: 9/40 tight-window releases restored 1-2 event
deltas past target (6,4)/(12,8 px, dot>0); 15/15 8 ms-split releases
showed exactly the 8 ms of accumulated deltas ((42..66),(28..44) px).
---
 hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
+++ b/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
@@ -998,6 +998,8 @@
     pointer_handle_axis_v120,
 };
 
+#define RELEASE_WARP_GRACE_US 8000
+
 static void
 relative_pointer_handle_relative_motion(void *data,
                                         struct zwp_relative_pointer_v1 *zwp_relative_pointer_v1,
@@ -1022,10 +1024,20 @@
      * active invalidates that target. Once the grab is released, freeze the
      * target across Xwayland's delayed cursor-show interval. For confinement
      * without an explicit grab, newer motion always invalidates it.
+     *
+     * Exceptions: games release capture with a warp+ungrab pair issued
+     * back-to-back in the same frame. At high input rates, motion which
+     * physically occurs inside the sub-millisecond window between the two
+     * requests, or which had already been delivered to the compositor while
+     * the warp was being processed, would falsely read as "newer" and break
+     * the pair's atomicity, restoring the drifted sprite instead of the
+     * warp target. Motion within a small grace window of the candidate
+     * warp is therefore not treated as newer.
      */
     if (warp_emulator &&
         warp_emulator->release_warp_valid &&
-        event_time_us > warp_emulator->release_warp_time_us &&
+        event_time_us >
+            warp_emulator->release_warp_time_us + RELEASE_WARP_GRACE_US &&
         (!warp_emulator->release_warp_was_grabbed ||
          xwl_seat_has_explicit_pointer_grab(xwl_seat)))
         warp_emulator->release_warp_valid = FALSE;
